1","F.Claude.2","I.Starshynov.1","M.Lewenstein.1","V.I.Kolobov.1","M.C.Braidotti.1","D.A.T.Vanzella.1","C.W.Gardiner.1","N.James.1","Theo.Torres.1","A.Prain.1","Jonathan.Sorce.1","F.Michel.1","G.Jannes.1","L.J.Garay.1","P.Maissa.2","A.Kumar.4","M.B.Plenio.1","S.Datta.16","G.Tricella.1","Daniele.Faccio.1","H.Kellay.1","J.Schmiedmayer.1","Demetrios.Christodoulou.1","M.Casals.2","N.Westerberg.1","R.M.Wald.1","I.B.Spielman.2","Z.Tian.1","I.Carusotto.1","A.Coutant.1","B.Reznik.1","S.Giorgini.1","George.E.A.Matsas.1","L.P.Pitaevskii.1","L.P.Euve.1","B.Damski.2","P.O.Fedichev.1","Piyush.Jain.1","G.K.Campbell.2","A.Retzker.1","Ofer.Lahav.1","M.Gutierrez.Galan.1","R.Balbinot.1","U.Moschella.1","M.C.J.Penrice.2","T.Bravo.1","P.Zoller.1","S.Finazzi.2","M.Novello.1","C.Mathis.2","Stephen.W.Hawking.1","S.J.Robertson.1","E.Giacobino.1","Soumen.Basak.1","Parthasarathi.Majumdar.1","S.T.Nguyen.1","Q.Glorieux.1","A.Bramati.1","R.Schutzhold.2","A.Lemaitre.3","D.Ratzel.1","K.Golubkov.2","M.J.Jacquet.1","A.V.Saa.1","J.R.Anglin.1","S.Erne.1","S.Banik.30","G.A.Lawrence.2","J.M.Salim.1","R.Penrose.1","V.E.Hubeny.1","T.G.Philbin.4","Ivette.Fuentes.1","S.Stringari.1","M.Joly.2","J.Louko.1","E.W.Tedford.2","D.Gerace.1","L.Giacomelli.1","A.Trombettoni.1","A.Blumkin.1","E.Goulart.1","J.Steinhauer.1","C.Gordon.5","R.A.da.Silva.1","Silke.Weinfurtner.1","F.Dalfovo.1","J.Macher.2","Junior.Diniz.Toniato.1","R.Parentani.1","G.Rousseaux.2","E.Galopin.1","S.Biermann.1","M.Richartz.1","M.A.AmoBaladron.1","Alessandro.Fabbri.1","M.C.H.Wright.1","S.Eckel.1","S.Patrick.2","G.E.Volovik.1","C.Barcelo.1","W.G.Unruh.1","D.Sanvitto.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"ok3.png","material":"preprint","caption":"{Behavior of $q_{\\mu\\nu}$ for the dispersive shock wave as background, found by numerically solving Eqs.~\\eqref{continuity}-\\eqref{rotationality}.} {We use here units in terms of a {length} $L$, chosen appropriately for the purpose of our numerical calculation. {For example, in the experiment \\cite{meppelink2009observation} the size of the condensate is roughly $500\\,\\mu m$, whereas the full x-axis range of our numerical simulation is 500. Therefore $L$ would be approximately $1\\,\\mu m$ with the parameters of Ref.~\\cite{meppelink2009observation}.} Time $t$ is then measured in units of $L^2$, when setting $\\hbar=1$. In these units,} the parameters we choose are $g=7500$, $\\rho_\\infty=0.002$, $\\eta=0.2$, $\\sigma=8.838$. {We then obtain $t_{\\rm shock}\\simeq 13.43$, using the method described in Ref.~\\cite{Damski2004}.} Only after and slightly before the instant of shock, the dispersive nature of the background flow becomes important in the oscillatory region, and hence in $q_{\\mu\\nu}$. Top left (at $t=27$): We plot $q_{tt}$ in the post-shock phase; the behavior of other metric components is similar. (Bottom row): In the post-shock phase, the amplitude of the parameter $\\alpha$ defined in Eq.~\\eqref{alpha} in the nonoscillatory region is essentially negligible compared to its amplitude in the oscillatory region; it however increases rapidly as $t$ approaches $t_{\\rm shock}$.","label":"fig1","source":"arxiv","key":"497f51656552903aab19af7c1300f779","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/497f51656552903aab19af7c1300f779"},{"filename":"equalarea2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"(Left) Multivalued solution of postshock Riemann wave by the method of characteristics (dotted), physical solution with discontinuity (nondispersive shock) by equal area rule \\cite{Landau1987Fluid} (solid line). (Right) Discontinuity in the flow is avoided as the wave approaches the shock time when we take quantum pressure into account. Inclusion of quantum pressure in the equation creates oscillation and thus continuous solution of $v_{(0)}$ removes singularity in the acoustic metric, i.e., censorship of singularity. The wave profile with quantum pressure is a good match with the nondispersive nonlinear wave profile in the region except the rapidly oscillatory region. Parameters as in Fig.~\\ref{fig1}.","label":"fig2","source":"arxiv","key":"eeebc7ce8ed1f07023e5cbf7202b8644","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/eeebc7ce8ed1f07023e5cbf7202b8644"},{"filename":"Ricci.png","material":"preprint","caption":"The Ricci scalar of $\\mathfrak{g}_{\\mu\\nu}$ approaching the shock singularity of the nondispersive Riemann wave.  Parameters are identical to those of Fig.~\\ref{fig1}.","label":"figr","source":"arxiv","key":"b89ff12de2a9167138419eb58abdfe5d","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b89ff12de2a9167138419eb58abdfe5d"},{"filename":"DiracDelta.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Sequences up to $n=4$ are shown to represent the delta distribution function (left) and its first derivative (right). $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{n \\to \\infty}}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)\\delta_n(x)dx=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)\\delta (x)dx$, and $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{n \\to \\infty}}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)\\delta'_n(x)dx=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)\\delta'(x)dx$ (for arbitrary $f(x)$) are used to define a relation of the Dirac delta distribution and its derivative with their respective sequence functions \\cite{arfken2013mathematical}.","label":"fig3","source":"arxiv","key":"34db32ef4e9503e96fa62f1bdb4ed4f7","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/34db32ef4e9503e96fa62f1bdb4ed4f7"},{"filename":"dtimeevol3.png","material":"preprint","caption":"(Top) Evolution of density profile with time. At $t=0$, the laser at the center of the condensate is switched off. The initial Gaussian density profile splits in two parts, moving in opposite directions, and an oscillation pattern is created, as described in Ref.~\\cite{Damski2004}. (Bottom) Zoomed-in view of the density profile in the oscillation region at $t=27$. Parameters as in Fig.~\\ref{fig1}.","label":"fig4","source":"arxiv","key":"cf34f72bd86762d03f04bc2f4f237eb7","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/cf34f72bd86762d03f04bc2f4f237eb7"},{"filename":"d2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"(Top) Evolution of density profile with time. At $t=0$, the laser at the center of the condensate is switched off. The initial Gaussian density profile splits in two parts, moving in opposite directions, and an oscillation pattern is created, as described in Ref.~\\cite{Damski2004}. (Bottom) Zoomed-in view of the density profile in the oscillation region at $t=27$. Parameters as in Fig.~\\ref{fig1}.","label":"fig4","source":"arxiv","key":"b528f0b80586c5585c932f6458739aef","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b528f0b80586c5585c932f6458739aef"},{"filename":"shocktimeZ2.png","material":"preprint","caption":"Density near the shock time, $t_{\\rm shock}\\simeq 13.43$. An oscillation starts instead of a discontinuity popping up, due to the presence of quantum pressure. The initial wave parameters are as in Fig.~\\ref{fig1}.","label":"fig7","source":"arxiv","key":"6f7ba07db8dcd16f3ddb88972fb961d9","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/6f7ba07db8dcd16f3ddb88972fb961d9"},{"filename":"vD2.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : ","label":"fig5","source":"arxiv","key":"b98edca2c2238236f12a7d372cf0004a","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b98edca2c2238236f12a7d372cf0004a"},{"filename":"cD2.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : ","label":"fig5","source":"arxiv","key":"76627d2a23fa78bead0b26959e57ec91","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/76627d2a23fa78bead0b26959e57ec91"},{"filename":"DD2.png","material":"preprint","caption":" : (Fig. (a) and Fig. (b)) We compare the contribution of the terms involving derivatives in $x$ in the 1D momentum equation Eq. \\eqref{euler} at $t=27$. We see that the quantum pressure term becomes significant only in the oscillatory region. (Fig. (c)) The quantum pressure is very much smaller, by several orders of magnitudes, in the nonoscillatory region. The parameters are identical to those in Fig.~\\ref{fig1}. : Caption not extracted","label":"fig5","source":"arxiv","key":"97780ae9b5f4606bfec2f98347451012","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/97780ae9b5f4606bfec2f98347451012"}],"inspire_categories":[{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology","source":"curator"},{"term":"Gravitation and Cosmology","source":"arxiv"}],"first_author":{"full_name":"Fischer, Uwe R.","last_name":"Fischer","first_name":"Uwe R.","recid":1009902},"control_number":2650751,"dois":[{"material":"publication","source":"APS","value":"10.1103/PhysRevD.107.084023"},{"material":"publication","source":"arXiv","value":"10.1103/PhysRevD.107.084023"}],"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Fischer:2022bkl","Fischer:2023fzn","Fischer:2022uki"],"deleted_records":[{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/2149236"}],"abstracts":[{"source":"APS","value":"A dispersionless shock wave in a fluid without friction develops an acoustic spacetime singularity which is naked (not hidden by a horizon). We show that this naked nondispersive shock-wave singularity is prohibited to form in a Bose-Einstein condensate, due to the microscopic structure of the underlying æther and the resulting effective trans-Planckian dispersion. Approaching the instant of shock <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>shock</mtext></msub></math>, rapid spatial oscillations of density and velocity develop around the shock location, which begin to emerge already slightly before <math display=\"inline\"><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>shock</mtext></msub></math>, due to the quantum pressure in the condensate. These oscillations render the acoustic spacetime structure completely regular, and therefore lead to a removal (censoring) of the spacetime singularity. Thus, distinct from the cosmic censorship hypothesis of Penrose formulated within Einsteinian gravity, the quantum pressure in Bose-Einstein condensates censors (prohibits) the formation of a naked shock-wave singularity, instead of hiding it behind a horizon.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"APS"}},{"source":"arXiv","value":"A dispersionless shock wave in a fluid without friction develops an acoustic spacetime singularity which is naked (not hidden by a horizon). We show that this naked nondispersive shock-wave singularity is prohibited to form in a Bose-Einstein condensate, due to the microscopic structure of the underlying ${\\rm a}\\!{\\rm e}$ther and the resulting effective trans-Planckian dispersion. Approaching the instant of shock $t_{\\rm shock}$, rapid spatial oscillations of density and velocity develop around the shock location, which begin to emerge already slightly before $t_{\\rm shock}$, due to the quantum pressure in the condensate. These oscillations render the acoustic spacetime structure completely regular, and therefore lead to a removal (censoring) of the spacetime singularity. Thus, distinct from the cosmic censorship hypothesis of Penrose formulated within Einsteinian gravity, the quantum pressure in Bose-Einstein condensates censors (prohibits) the formation of a naked shock-wave singularity, instead of hiding it behind a horizon.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["gr-qc"],"titles":[{"source":"APS","title":"Dispersive censor of acoustic spacetimes with a shock-wave singularity"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Cosmic censor of shock-wave singularities"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"The cosmic censor of shock-wave singularities"},{"source":"arXiv","title":"Dispersive censor of acoustic spacetimes with a shock-wave singularity"}],"imprints":[{"date":"2023-04-12"}],"curated":false}}],"total":2},"links":{"self":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...Naked%20singularity%20censoring...&size=10&page=1","bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...Naked%20singularity%20censoring...&size=10&page=1&format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...Naked%20singularity%20censoring...&size=10&page=1&format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...Naked%20singularity%20censoring...&size=10&page=1&format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...Naked%20singularity%20censoring...&size=10&page=1&format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...Naked%20singularity%20censoring...&size=10&page=1&format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=...Naked%20singularity%20censoring...&size=10&page=1&format=cv"}}